{
  "id": "u06-l02",
  "unit": "u06-bazaar",
  "title": "Taka & prices",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Prices are just **number + টাকা** (*taka*): **পঞ্চাশ টাকা** 'fifty taka', **একশ টাকা** 'a hundred taka'. With no 'is' in the present, **চা দশ টাকা** simply means 'Tea (is) ten taka'.\n\nHundreds are built with **-শ** (*-sho*): **একশ** 100, **দুইশ** 200, **পাঁচশ** 500. A thousand is **হাজার** — **এক হাজার টাকা** = ৳1,000.\n\nA taka is 100 **পয়সা** (*poysha*). You'll rarely handle poysha coins today, but the word survives in expressions about money.\n\n**Listening tip:** Bengali numbers are irregular, so shopkeepers' quick answers are the hardest part of the bazaar. Don't be shy to ask again: **কত?**",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "taka",
      "bn": "টাকা",
      "tr": "taka",
      "en": "taka",
      "notes": "Bangladesh's currency (৳, BDT). Also the general word for 'money': টাকা নেই 'no money'. In India টাকা means rupees."
    },
    {
      "id": "poysha",
      "bn": "পয়সা",
      "tr": "poysha",
      "en": "poisha",
      "notes": "1/100 of a taka. Coins are now rare, but you hear it in sayings and in counting."
    },
    {
